<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/constitutions-rights">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A constitution is a country's operating system: the rules about making rules. Its two core
    moves are <strong>separating power</strong> so no one holds all of it, and
    <strong>entrenching rights</strong> so majorities cannot casually steamroll minorities.
    Constitutions come written (USA, India, most countries) or unwritten (the UK's accretion of
    statutes and conventions) — and rigid (hard to amend) or flexible (easy).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from Montesquieu to judicial review — five steps of restrained power" widget="equationStep" config={sepCfg} />

  <h2>What rights actually do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Negative vs positive.</strong> Most classic rights are "thou shalt not" constraints on the state (speech, religion); newer social rights are obligations to provide (education, healthcare). They cost different things — which is why constitutions treat them differently.</li>
    <li><strong>Rights collide.</strong> Free speech vs defamation; protest vs public order. Courts resolve collisions with proportionality: is the limit lawful, necessary and the least restrictive option?</li>
    <li><strong>Amendment is the pressure valve.</strong> The US Constitution has 27 amendments in 235 years (and its most important, the 14th, rewrote citizenship after a civil war); India's has been amended over a hundred times. Rigid constitutions trade agility for stability.</li>
    <li><strong>International floor.</strong> The 1948 Universal Declaration is not itself a treaty, but it seeded binding covenants (1966) and every modern human-rights court.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The warning label</h2>
  <p>
    Paper constraints fail when the actors agree to ignore them. Political scientists watch for
    the soft attacks: packing courts, capturing referees, changing rules after each election. A
    constitution is only as strong as the willingness to lose by it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Montesquieu, The Spirit of the Laws (1748); Marbury v. Madison (1803); UN Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948).</p>
</TopicPage>
